<local:JJFlexDialog x:Class="JJFlexWpf.Dialogs.RemoveRadioDialog"
                    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:local="clr-namespace:JJFlexWpf"
                    Title="Remove Radio"
                    Width="520"
                    SizeToContent="Height"
                    WindowStartupLocation="CenterScreen"
                    ShowInTaskbar="False"
                    ResizeMode="NoResize">
    <!-- ONE dialog with a scope choice, not two menu items (task #98, shape
         ratified by Noel). One thing to find; the consequences sit next to the
         choice; the safe scope is the default and is checked before the
         operator touches anything.

         Focus lands in the body text, not on a button and not on the radio
         group — the same conservative posture as ConfirmActionDialog, and for
         the same reason it was adopted there in 2026-08-04: the warnings are
         the highest-stakes text in the flow, and focus starting in them is
         what makes a screen reader read them before anything is decided. A
         read-only TextBox is also the only one of these controls that can be
         re-read line by line with the arrow keys.

         The scope radios carry their consequence in their accessible NAME
         rather than in a TextBlock beside them. That looks like the mistake
         task #107 just finished undoing, and it is the opposite: a TextBlock
         is invisible to a screen reader on focus, this is a one-time
         irreversible decision, and the two options differ in exactly the way
         the name states. Speak what the UI does not otherwise convey. -->
    <StackPanel Margin="16">
        <TextBox x:Name="BodyText"
                 IsReadOnly="True"
                 IsReadOnlyCaretVisible="True"
                 TextWrapping="Wrap"
                 AcceptsReturn="True"
                 VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                 MaxHeight="300"
                 Margin="0,0,0,12"
                 AutomationProperties.Name="What removing this radio does, read only"/>
        <!-- "read only" IS IN THE NAME ON PURPOSE (Noel, 2026-08-20). The box
             genuinely is read-only, but a TextBox still announces as "edit", so
             it sounds changeable. The only true fix is swapping the
             AutomationPeer — and that sacrifices the arrow-key line-by-line
             reading this dialog depends on, and would be systemic anyway: about
             45 instances across 24 files.
             This is a WORKAROUND, labelled as one. It costs one attribute, keeps
             arrow-key reading, and gets the constraint spoken before the wrong
             role is. If the peer question is ever solved properly, take this out
             rather than leaving both. -->

        <!-- Own navigation group so the arrows wrap inside the pair instead of
             walking out into the buttons. Same fix as the SmartLink mode radios
             on 2026-08-18, where arrowing past the last option landed the
             operator in a port field.

             TabNavigation is Continue, NOT Once, and that is deliberate (task
             #132). "Once" gives the whole group a single tab stop, so Tab landed
             on the pre-checked safe option and the next Tab went straight to the
             Remove button — the destructive option was never ENCOUNTERED by an
             operator who tabs, only by one who knew to arrow. Confirming then
             committed the default after an interaction that felt complete. That
             happened to Noel on 2026-08-19; nothing in the code was wrong, the
             navigation was. "Once" is textbook-correct WPF and right on a
             settings page with many groups, where the operator is arrowing
             anyway. In a dialog whose entire purpose is one irreversible choice
             between two options, the assumption it makes becomes load-bearing.

             The pre-checked safe default STAYS. It is load-bearing for a
             separate deliberate decision (RigSelectorDialog.xaml.cs, the Delete
             key handler): bare Delete with no modifier is defensible as a
             keypress precisely because this confirmation's default scope deletes
             nothing. Reachability was the defect, not the default. -->
        <StackPanel KeyboardNavigation.DirectionalNavigation="Cycle"
                    KeyboardNavigation.TabNavigation="Continue"
                    Margin="0,0,0,8">
            <RadioButton x:Name="ScopeListOnlyRadio"
                         GroupName="RemoveScope"
                         IsChecked="True"
                         Margin="0,4,0,4"
                         Content="Remove from the list only"
                         AutomationProperties.Name="Remove from the list only. Everything you have set up for this radio is kept."
                         local:JJFlexHelp.Text="The radio stops appearing in your list, and nothing is deleted. If it turns up again — you switch it on, or your SmartLink account lists it — it comes back with every setting exactly as you left it. That also means removing a radio that is online right now barely does anything, because the next look for radios finds it. This is the one to use for a leftover entry that will never answer again."/>
            <RadioButton x:Name="ScopeEverythingRadio"
                         GroupName="RemoveScope"
                         Margin="0,4,0,4"
                         Content="Remove the radio and its settings"
                         AutomationProperties.Name="Remove the radio and its settings. Everything you have set up for this radio is deleted and cannot be recovered."
                         local:JJFlexHelp.Text="Deletes this radio's whole folder of settings. The radio itself can come back the moment something sees it again — the configuration cannot, and there is no undo. If the radio is still real and you simply want it out of the way, choose the other option instead."/>
        </StackPanel>

        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" HorizontalAlignment="Right" Margin="0,8,0,0">
            <Button x:Name="OkButton"
                    Content="_Remove"
                    MinWidth="90" Height="28" Margin="0,0,8,0"
                    IsDefault="False"
                    AutomationProperties.AcceleratorKey="Alt+R"
                    Click="OkButton_Click"/>
            <Button x:Name="CancelButton"
                    Content="_Cancel"
                    MinWidth="90" Height="28"
                    IsCancel="True"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Cancel"
                    AutomationProperties.AcceleratorKey="Alt+C"
                    Click="CancelButton_Click"/>
        </StackPanel>
    </StackPanel>
</local:JJFlexDialog>
